<blockquote class="uncited"><div>Interesting. What kind of datastructure has O(1) update ? and O(log(n)) complexity for long raycast ? <br><br>If it is a tree, like a loose kdop tree, after some motion, the tree might become very unbalanced, which makes the O(log(n)) not valid anymore. Do you rebalance the tree ?</div></blockquote>I'm using a loose octree. As far as I know it's still O(log n) after some motion. Actually I don't think there's a difference between what you get after some motion and what you would get by re-building the octree from scratch at this point.<br><br>- Pierre<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=76">Pierre</a> — Tue Jul 26, 2005 1:59 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
