		<author><name><![CDATA[Erwin Coumans]]></name></author>
		<updated>2006-11-12T00:26:57+00:00</updated>

		<published>2005-07-25T14:46:04+00:00</published>
		<id>https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=118#p118</id>
		<link href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=118#p118"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[raycast through the sweep and prune space]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=118#p118"><![CDATA[
Although Gino van den Bergen's explanation of my idea is better and much more detailed, here is the original posting on comp.graphics.algorithms:<br><br><a href="http://www.erwincoumans.com/p/raycast_on_sweep_and_prune.pdf" class="postlink">http://www.erwincoumans.com/p/raycast_o ... _prune.pdf</a><br><br>It is a very efficient way to do raycasting on dynamics scenes. It works best for very long rays (bullets), that do have a hit. See the detailed description in "Collision Detection in Interactive 3D Environments".<br><br>For very short ray, it is better to add its aabb inside the Sweep and Prune space, and perform the raycast on the overlapping objects of that aabb.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=2">Erwin Coumans</a> — Mon Jul 25, 2005 2:46 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
